    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "When a new owner's computed customer code is already taken, it now gets `-2`, `-3`, \u2026 appended, using the smallest number from 2 up that's free. The de-duplicated code is what gets saved and returned. The full test suite passes: 17 tests, including 3 new ones.\n\n**What changed:**\n- **Lookup query:** `OwnerRepository` has a new `countByCustomerCode(String)`. I implemented it in all three persistence variants (Spring Data, JPA and JDBC).\n- **Service:** in `ClinicServiceImpl.saveOwner`, a new `uniqueCustomerCode(...)` keeps the computed code if nobody has it, and otherwise tries suffixes from 2 upward. This only runs when an owner is first created, so updating an owner never changes their code.\n- **Region fix in `OwnerMapper.toLocality`:** it found the region by cutting the code at the *last* dash. With a suffix, `NSW-1A2B3C4D-2` would have given the region `NSW-1A2B3C4D`. It now cuts at the *first* dash, which is safe because no region name contains a dash.\n- **New test:** `CustomerCodeCollisionTests` uses a fake repository to force real collisions. It checks that a unique code stays as it is, that the first collision gets `-2`, and that the smallest free number is chosen (`-4` when `-2` and `-3` are taken). The existing acceptance test can't create a real collision through the API, which is why I added this.\n\n**Limitations:**\n- **Simultaneous sign-ups:** nothing in the database stops two owners from getting the same code if both are created at the same moment. A unique constraint on `customer_code` would close that gap.\n- **Column length:** `customer_code` is `VARCHAR(20)` in the H2, HSQLDB and MySQL schemas. The longest code (`UNKNOWN-XXXXXXXX`, 16 characters) has room for suffixes up to `-999`.\n- **Derived values:** the membership number and check digit are built from the customer code, so they include the suffix too.",
